issue.body:
While most types maintain consistency between `equals()` and `hashCode()` after string serialization and parsing (`JsonElement#toString()` + `JsonParser#parse(String)`, integers do not. `equals()` returns true, but the hash codes are different.

```
  public void testEqualsIntegerAndLazilyParsedInteger() {
    MoreAsserts.assertEqualsAndHashCode(new JsonPrimitive(42), new JsonPrimitive(new LazilyParsedNumber("42")));
  }
```

The internal method `JsonReaderInternalAccess.promoteNameToValue(JsonReader)` which is used before the key of a `Map<K,V>` is read causes an incorrect JSON path because after the name has been read as value, the JSON path is not updated.

For example:
```java
String malformedJson = "{\"name\":\"value";
try {
    new Gson().fromJson(malformedJson, new TypeToken<Map<String, String>>() {}.getType());
} catch (JsonSyntaxException exception) {
    // Incorrect: Path is missing name
    // Unterminated string at line 1 column 15 path $.
    System.out.println(exception.getCause().getMessage());
}

JsonReader jsonReader = new JsonReader(new StringReader(malformedJson));
jsonReader.beginObject();
jsonReader.nextName();
try {
    jsonReader.nextString();
} catch (MalformedJsonException exception) {
    // Correct: Path includes name
    // Unterminated string at line 1 column 15 path $.name
    System.out.println(exception.getMessage());
}
```

I am serializing a list like this:
```
List<? extends TestBeanBase> list1;
list1.add(new TestBeanSub());    // TestBeanSub is a child class of TestBeanBase
```
I find that the fields of TestBeanSub is missed in the serialized json.

You can try my example code：
```java
    @Test
    public void testList() {
        List<TestBeanBase> list = new ArrayList<>();

        //base class
        TestBeanBase baseBean = new TestBeanBase();
        baseBean.a = "1";
        list.add(baseBean);

        //sub class
        TestBeanSub subBean = new TestBeanSub();
        subBean.a = "1";
        subBean.b = "2";//b is a field declared in sub class, this field value is missed during serialization
        list.add(subBean);

        TestBeanList beanList = new TestBeanList();
        beanList.list1 = list;

        Gson gson = new GsonBuilder().setPrettyPrinting().create();
        System.out.println(gson.toJson(beanList));
    }

    public static class TestBeanList {
        public List<? extends TestBeanBase> list1; //element type is a WildcardType
    }

    public static class TestBeanBase {
        public String a;
    }

    public static class TestBeanSub extends TestBeanBase {
        public String b;
    }
```

Output json :
```
{
  "list1": [
    {
      "a": "1"
    },
    {
      "a": "1"   //field b is missed
    }
  ]
}
```

**The problem**

`JsonReader.readEscapeCharacter()` ([`JsonReader.java:1855`](https://github.com/google/gson/blob/main/gson/src/main/java/com/google/gson/stream/JsonReader.java#L1855)) reads 4 hex digits, casts to `(char)`, and returns unchecked at [line 1882](https://github.com/google/gson/blob/main/gson/src/main/java/com/google/gson/stream/JsonReader.java#L1882). No `Character.isSurrogate` / `0xD800..0xDFFF` gate anywhere. Callers at [line 1244](https://github.com/google/gson/blob/main/gson/src/main/java/com/google/gson/stream/JsonReader.java#L1244) (string-value) and [line 1340](https://github.com/google/gson/blob/main/gson/src/main/java/com/google/gson/stream/JsonReader.java#L1340) (skip-string) consume the raw char with no validation. Both `setLenient(false)` and `Strictness.STRICT` accept the malformed input.

**The specs**

- [RFC 8259 §8.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8259.html#section-8.2): "behavior of software that receives JSON texts containing such values is unpredictable; for example, implementations might return different values for the length of a string value or even suffer fatal runtime exceptions."
- [RFC 7493 §2.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7493.html#section-2.1) (I-JSON): "Object member names, and string values in arrays and object members, MUST NOT include code points that identify Surrogates or Noncharacters as defined by [UNICODE]... `\uDEAD` is invalid because it is an unpaired surrogate."
- [RFC 8785 §3.2.2.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8785.html#section-3.2.2.2) (JCS): "occurrences of such data MUST cause a compliant JCS implementation to terminate with an appropriate error."

**Reproducer (gson 2.14.0, JDK 21, `Strictness.STRICT`)**

```java
import com.google.gson.*;
import com.google.gson.stream.*;
import java.io.StringReader;

public class Repro3113 {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        String[] docs = {"{\"k\":\"\\uD800\"}", "{\"k\":\"\\uDC00\"}", "{\"\\uDC00\":1}"};
        for (String doc : docs) {
            JsonReader r = new JsonReader(new StringReader(doc));
            r.setStrictness(Strictness.STRICT);
            r.beginObject();
            String name = r.nextName();
            String val = r.peek() == JsonToken.NUMBER
                    ? String.valueOf(r.nextInt()) : r.nextString();
            System.out.println("input=" + doc
                + " ACCEPT name.char0=0x" + Integer.toHexString(name.charAt(0))
                + " val.char0=0x" + Integer.toHexString(val.charAt(0)));
        }
    }
}
```

Output:
```
input={"k":"\uD800"} ACCEPT name.char0=0x6b val.char0=0xd800
input={"k":"\uDC00"} ACCEPT name.char0=0x6b val.char0=0xdc00
input={"\uDC00":1}  ACCEPT name.char0=0xdc00 val.char0=0x31
```

**How to read the output**

- `input=` shows the JSON document fed in.
- `ACCEPT` means the parser returned successfully (no exception).
- `name.char0` is the hex value of the first char of the field name.
- `val.char0` is the hex value of the first char of the value.
- Row 1: field name is `"k"` (`0x6b`), value's first char is `0xd800` — a lone high surrogate returned as-is in the Java `String`.
- Row 2: same field name, value's first char is `0xdc00` — a lone low surrogate returned as-is.
- Row 3: field NAME itself starts with `0xdc00` (a lone low surrogate); the value is just the digit `1` (`0x31`).

All three should have rejected under `Strictness.STRICT` per the RFC 7493 / RFC 8785 requirements above.

**Suggested fix**

The return at [`JsonReader.java:1882`](https://github.com/google/gson/blob/main/gson/src/main/java/com/google/gson/stream/JsonReader.java#L1882) is the site to add the guard. Rejecting under `Strictness.STRICT` preserves current behavior in `Strictness.LEGACY_STRICT` / `Strictness.LENIENT` — no Android-compat break.

The type adapter included by default for `java.net.URI` does not preserve the identity of all URI values. In other words:

```java
URI uri = new URI("s3://bucket/path/Kankyō.png");
URI roundTripUri = gson.fromJson(gson.toJson(uri), URI.class);
assert roundTripUri.equals(uri); // AssertionError
```

The problem is that the implementation [here](https://github.com/google/gson/blob/d1828736eb425a575667e0c86117ced787d1cf5b/gson/src/main/java/com/google/gson/internal/bind/TypeAdapters.java#L502-L520) uses `URI#toASCIIString`, which does not preserve the original URI components but instead normalizes them like this:

```java
Normalizer.normalize(s, Normalizer.Form.NFC)
```

This is very bad, because it is changing the meaning of the URI on serialization. This issue has caused numerous bugs at our business dealing with international filenames being uploaded to our SAAS products and then being changed after being passed between multiple microservices.

Here is an alternate implementation that we have started to use in our code:

```java
public class NonNormalizingUriTypeAdapter extends TypeAdapter<URI> {
    @Override
    public URI read(JsonReader in) throws IOException {
        if (in.peek() == JsonToken.NULL) {
            in.nextNull();
            return null;
        }
        try {
            String nextString = in.nextString();
            return "null".equals(nextString) ? null : new URI(nextString);
        }
        catch (URISyntaxException e) {
            throw new JsonIOException(e);
        }
    }

    @Override
    public void write(JsonWriter out, URI value) throws IOException {
        out.value(value == null ? null : value.toString());
    }
}
```

Really the only difference is using `toString()` instead of `toASCIIString()`, which URL-encodes the result without changing the character byte representations.
